cat > 'README.md' <<'OC_EOF_4'
# OpenCode Cockpit

One calendar workspace and one backend process for running a small team of [OpenCode](https://opencode.ai) agents on your own
machine. You issue one order. A lead model cuts it into sub-orders, other models do the parts, a judge program takes or
refuses what they hand in, and every step is a row in an append-only ledger that the page is drawn from.

- **OpenCode only.** The one agent command this backend ever starts is `opencode run --format json -m <provider/model>`.
- **One model per seat.** Which seat runs which model is a small JSON file. DeepSeek, Kimi and Qwen have their own
  icons; any other model gets a neutral one (see [ICONS.md](ICONS.md)).
- **Nothing to install besides OpenCode.** The backend is Python standard library only; the page is plain HTML, CSS and
  JavaScript with no build step, and it fetches nothing from another host.
- **Local.** The backend listens on `127.0.0.1` and answers only the page it serves.
- **A desktop page**, white, in English. There is no phone layout.

## What has and has not been tested

Read this before you rely on it.

- Tested: the dispatcher, the git layer and the earlier cockpit end to end with a **stand-in** for the `opencode`
  executable (`tests/fake_bin/opencode`). The calendar tests use the real request handlers, ledger writer and stand-in
  processes without sockets; they cover dates, history, plan persistence, cancellation, exactly-once issuance, lead-only
  HTTP orders, unchanged ledger bytes on rejection, and each run's saved lead and order issuers.
- The library tests use the same handlers, ledger writer, git blobs and stand-in without sockets. They cover saved
  bytes, refused saves, restarts, historical backfill, launch context, usage receipts, proposals and human review,
  atomic review failure, lessons, context limits, and workflow metrics counted independently from the ledger.
  **Whether a real model plans better or costs less with this library has not been measured.**
- The page has static checks and a Node DOM stand-in for routes, both order forms, calendar refresh, links, previews,
  lead identities, sub-order dispatchers, seat order titles, Library filters, file saves, review validation, metrics,
  live state changes, preserved drafts and focused controls, empty states, and flow edges avoiding unrelated nodes.
  Static checks compute text contrast from the CSS tokens and check control states and seat link truncation.
  **There is no browser test in this repository.** Geometry, actual contrast after rendering, animation, scroll
  anchoring and the layout at 1280 by 900 still need browser measurement. The socket-based cockpit tests need
  loopback binding. In the current restricted run, calendar (9), dispatcher (41), library (11), page (18), and
  privacy (14) tests passed; seven of the 12 cockpit tests could not bind their socket (`Operation not permitted`).
  The other five cockpit tests passed. Run the full suite below in an environment that permits loopback binding.
- **Not tested: a real OpenCode process with a real model.** The command line, the environment switches and the event
  shapes read here follow notes taken on opencode-ai 1.18.32; they have not been checked again against a newer release.
- The model ids and the key variable names in `team.example.json` are examples, not verified values. Run
  `opencode models` to see what your installation calls its models, and check your provider's documentation for the
  name of its key variable.

## Try it without a model

Needs Python 3.9 or newer and git 2.28 or newer.

    python3 cockpit/serve.py ../opencode-runs --opencode tests/fake_bin/opencode

Open <http://127.0.0.1:8800/>, type a title and a goal on Home, and press **Send to lead**. The stand-in plans two sub-orders
and writes one small file for each; nothing is sent anywhere and nothing costs anything.

## One file

`opencode-cockpit.sh` in the repository root holds every other tracked file. Copy that one file to a machine, and

    bash opencode-cockpit.sh [FOLDER]

writes the whole tree into FOLDER (default `opencode-cockpit`) and checks the SHA-256 of each file it wrote. It needs
bash and `sha256sum` or `shasum`; it calls no git and opens no connection. Every file sits in it as a plain
here-document, so it can be read before it is run. It refuses a folder that already holds files and writes nothing
outside FOLDER. The folder it makes is not a git checkout and has no history; running the cockpit there still needs
Python and git as above.

The file is generated: after changing any tracked file run `python3 tools/make_oneshot.py`, or `tests/test_oneshot.py`
fails. That test runs the script into a temporary folder and compares every file. The script has been run with bash 3.2
on macOS only; it has not been tried on Linux or any other system.

## Run it with OpenCode

1. Install OpenCode and make sure `opencode` is on your `PATH` (or pass `--opencode /path/to/opencode`).
2. Copy `team.example.json` to `team.json` and put in the models you want, written `provider/model`.
3. Export your provider keys in the shell that starts the backend, and list the *names* of those variables under
   `pass_env` in the team file. Keys are never written to a file by this project.
4. Start it:

       python3 cockpit/serve.py ../opencode-runs --team team.json

Choose a runs root outside this source repository. Every start makes a new folder under that root (ledger, git
repository, sandboxes, logs). The backend refuses a root inside this repository so its shared library cannot enter
the source tree. Ctrl-C stops the backend; an
agent that is still running then is ended and what it produced is not collected.

| Option | Default | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `--team FILE` | `team.example.json` | which seat runs which model |
| `--port N` | `8800` | the port on `127.0.0.1` |
| `--max-launches N` | `12` | how many agent processes one start may run in all; the first refusal stops everything after it |
| `--wall SECONDS` | `600` | one agent process is ended after this long (a rate-limited model makes `opencode run` hang silently) |
| `--judge FILE` | the floor judge | a judge of your own (see below) |
| `--opencode PATH` | `$OPENCODE_BIN`, else `opencode` | the executable to start |
| `--clock-offset SECONDS` | `0` | shift the shared ledger, run-name and local calendar clock for rehearsals; negative values work too |

Each order is also limited to two launches. Model cost is whatever OpenCode reports in its `step_finish` events; the
launch table shows it, and this project sets no spending limit of its own beyond the launch counts.

## Using the page

Home shows today's orders, running orders, orders needing attention, and the live run's launch count. Type a title
and goal in its compose card, then press **Send to lead** or Cmd/Ctrl+Enter. The order goes to the named lead with
the same default write scope, budget of six, and judge rules as today's day form. An empty title or goal keeps the
draft and explains what is missing. While sending, the button is busy and a second submission is ignored. Success
opens the new order automatically and shows a confirmation for five seconds.

The Team row on Home shows each seat's model, working or idle state, launch count, and last order.
Recent activity turns the live ledger into up to 12 plain sentences, newest first, with order links and times.

The order page follows its state, sub-orders and seats as they change. Its metrics show launches, sent-back work
and seconds. While work is running these are estimates from its launches (seconds sum the durations reported so
far); a finished top-level order uses its stored workflow metrics, whose seconds measure elapsed time from issue
to completion. File rows keep the preview action. Press **Keep as skill** for reusable instructions or **Keep as
reference** for material a later order can consult. The row changes to a Library link immediately. Files from
sub-orders can also be kept from their parent's page, with the file's own source order preserved.

Open **Library** in the left navigation to see every entry, including rejected and superseded versions. Filter by
skill, artifact, workflow or lesson. Pending proposals appear first; an entry opens its kept text, version, usage,
source order, replacement link, and any workflow steps or lesson reason. A proposed skill has an immediately
visible review bar: **Accept skill** makes it available to future launches; **Reject proposal** requires a reason
and records that reason as a lesson. A proposal reaches no seat before a person accepts it.
Past-run order pages stay read-only; **Keep in the Library** opens a Library form for the file's title, kind and optional tags.

The Library's **What each order cost** chart and table list finished orders from oldest to newest, with launches,
sent-back counts, elapsed seconds and what the lead used. A dot and the word "library" mark reuse in the chart.
An order's own Library block links to what the lead used and to proposals awaiting review. These measures make
reuse and changes in launch counts visible; they do not establish that a real model is better or cheaper.

Views refresh without replacing unchanged data. Existing controls are reconciled in place to keep drafts,
selection and focus; initial loads show skeleton blocks. The day form keeps its existing behavior: after sending,
it stays on that day and offers an **Open order** link.

## Calendar and planning

Open the calendar icon in the top bar, then choose a day. Today can issue an order immediately; a later day can save
a plan; an earlier day's form is closed. A plan becomes due on its date, but **only pressing Issue now starts its
order**. It never starts just because time passed. Waiting and due plans can be cancelled.

History spans every run folder under the same runs root. Orders belong to the local day of their issue event; day
totals count top-level orders, and launch totals include their sub-orders. Open an order from any view to see the same
flow, models, launches, files and ledger. Past runs are read-only. Unreadable run ledgers are reported, not hidden.
Each new run pins its team configuration in `team.json`; older runs can use their saved `logs/team.json`.

**Every order goes through the lead.** A person's order goes to the running team's lead, who plans the parts and hands
them to the other seats; the judge decides whether their work passes. `POST /message` rejects an `order.issue` unless
`body.to` is that lead actor, returning 400 `{"cli": "not-through-lead", "detail": "..."}` before the dispatcher sees it,
with the ledger bytes unchanged. Other envelope kinds and sub-orders issued internally from the lead's plan keep
their existing behavior. The day form and order cards identify the lead by name, model and icon.

Plans live in the append-only `planned.jsonl` at the runs root, outside all run ledgers. Issuance uses the same
`order.issue` envelope and ledger writer as the day form, under the server lock. Repeating an issue request returns
the existing run and order; recovery also checks for an order written before an interrupted plan receipt.

The left rail opens Home, Calendar, Board, Team, Files, Ledger and Library. Home lists what needs attention, today's
orders, the team and upcoming planned days. The board spans all runs. Team, Files and Ledger show the live run. The question
mark opens Help, whose examples fill today's form without sending it. Hash routes support Back and Forward.

The endpoints use the same host and origin gate as `/message`:

| Endpoint | Result |
|---|---|
| `GET /state` | the live ledger and folded state |
| `GET /team` | live seats, models, launches and files |
| `GET /history` | server `today`, `live_run`, runs, days with orders/plans/stats, each order's `lead`, and skipped runs with reasons |
| `GET /order?run=RUN&id=ORDER` | `lead`, order fields, direct children, descendant flow nodes, seats, launches, files and ledger; orders include `by` |
| `GET /file?path=KEY&run=RUN` | handed-in file preview; omitting `run` selects the live run |
| `POST /message` | a dispatcher envelope; the day form sends `order.issue` |
| `POST /plan` | save `{title, goal, writes, budget, day}` for a real future date; returns `{id, state: "waiting"}` |
| `POST /plan/issue` | `{id}` issues a due plan; returns its run and order; repeats return 409 `already-issued`, waiting returns 409 `not-due` |
| `POST /plan/cancel` | `{id}` cancels a waiting or due plan; closed plans return 409 |

Invalid plans return 400 `bad-plan`. Unknown runs or orders return 404. Run ids must identify a direct folder under
the runs root; traversal and symlinks are refused. File previews resolve ledger artifact keys, never arbitrary disk
paths. The page refreshes state and history every second, using the backend's local date rather than the browser's date.

`GET /order` includes `lead: {actor, name, model, family}` from that run's saved team, and `by` on `order`, every
`children` item and every flow node, taken from its `order.issued` ledger row. Each day's `orders` items in
`GET /history` carry the same `lead` object for their own run, even when the live team has changed. If a historical
run has no saved team, the lead fields are empty strings; `GET /history` reports unreadable team records in `skipped`.

## The library and the lead's loop

The shared library lives at `<runs root>/library/`, outside the source repository and outside every run. It survives
restarts and is available to later runs using the same root. It is not listed as a run. An atomic snapshot stores
entry metadata, original bytes and processing receipts together; a filesystem lock serializes writers from different
runs. The library adds no model calls.

- A person can keep any file listed by `GET /order` as an **artifact** (reference material) or a **skill** (UTF-8
  instructions for seats). Both are accepted immediately. Saving the same run, order, path and kind again returns
  the original entry. Its `sha256` and `bytes` describe the exact handed-in blob, even if a later order changed that
  path. Tags are text strings; titles have 1 to 200 characters. Artifact previews decode binary data with replacement
  characters, but the stored bytes and digest remain exact.
- The backend records one **workflow** per finished top-level order, including success, failure, exhausted leases
  and cancellation. It backfills older runs on startup. Steps contain all descendant sub-orders in issue order.
  Metrics count `lease.granted` rows in the family, leases beyond the first for each order (`sent_back`), and seconds
  from issue to the terminal ledger event. `sent_back` also counts retries unrelated to a judge's refusal.
- A **lesson** preserves each judge refusal or terminal failure, its order, seat and reason. Exhausted leases get a
  backend explanation because there are no judge words for that event. Rejecting a proposal also records a lesson.

Every seat receives `_dispatch/LIBRARY.json`, even when empty. Its `v` is `library/1`; it contains accepted skills
with their text, accepted artifact summaries and sources, the 10 newest workflows, and the 20 newest lessons. Only
the newest accepted skill in each replacement chain is included. Proposed, rejected and superseded entries never
reach seats. The entire JSON document is at most 64 KB: oldest entries are dropped first across all kinds, as whole
entries. A skill too large to fit is kept on disk but omitted from context. The dispatch channel never counts as
handed-in work or as an out-of-scope change.

The lead's rules require it to read the library before planning, reuse what fits, and name entry ids in the optional
`uses` array beside `orders` in `_dispatch/plan.json`. Known ids count once per accepted plan, with duplicate ids
ignored; later replanning can count another use. Unknown ids are retained in the workflow's `unknown_uses`, but do
not increment a counter. Workers are told to follow the listed skills that fit their order.

The lead may also include `propose`, a list of up to three objects:

    {"kind": "skill", "title": "Review checklist", "text": "Check the inputs before writing.",
     "replaces": null, "why": "Avoid repeat launches."}

Each valid object among the first three becomes a proposed skill, attributed to the lead actor and its order.
Text must be nonempty UTF-8 of at most 8 KB. A replacement must name an existing skill and gets its version plus one;
a new skill starts at version one. Malformed proposals are ignored without changing how the git layer judges the
plan. Only a plan that issued sub-orders contributes uses or proposals. A person must accept a proposal before it
reaches seats; accepting supersedes its named predecessor. Rejecting requires a nonempty reason.

All library routes use the existing host and origin gate. Errors include `cli` and `detail`.

| Endpoint | Result |
|---|---|
| `GET /library` | `{entries, counts}`; metadata without text, newest first; counts for artifact, skill, workflow and lesson |
| `GET /library/item?id=ID` | Entry plus `text`, at most 64 KB; 404 `unknown-entry` |
| `POST /library/save` | `{run, order, path, kind, title, tags}` returns `{entry}`; kind is artifact or skill |
| `POST /library/review` | `{id, decision, reason}` returns `{entry}`; decision is accept or reject; only proposals are reviewable |
| `GET /library/context` | Exactly the document the next launch receives, provided no intervening library change occurs |
| `GET /library/efficiency` | `{orders: [{run, order, title, ended, state, launches, sent_back, seconds, uses}]}`, oldest completion first |

`GET /order` adds `library: {workflow, uses, proposed, saved}`. `workflow` is its workflow id or null; `uses` contains
`{id, kind, title}` objects; `proposed` contains pending entries from the order's family; `saved` maps file paths to
saved entry ids. When a path has both kinds, the most recently created saved entry is shown there; both remain in
the library. Times are UTC ISO strings, and ids are readable kind/title/counter slugs of at most 48 characters.

A save accepts only a file listed for that run and order (including its descendants). Traversal, absolute paths,
unknown runs and files belonging only to another order return 400 `not-a-handed-in-file`, with nothing written.
Reviewing a non-proposal returns 400 `not-reviewable`; rejecting without a reason returns 400 `reason-needed`.

For deterministic rehearsals, the stand-in accepts `FAKE_OPENCODE_LIBRARY`, a JSON object with `uses` and `propose`
fields, when its name is in the team's `pass_env`. Setting `uses` to `"context"` names every entry it receives.
With the variable unset, its existing two-sub-order behavior stays unchanged. These tests measure the plumbing,
not the quality or cost of a real model's plans.

## The team file

    {
      "lead":  {"id": "lead", "name": "Lead", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-chat"},
      "seats": [
        {"id": "writer",   "name": "Writer",   "model": "moonshotai/kimi-k2"},
        {"id": "reviewer", "name": "Reviewer", "model": "alibaba/qwen3-coder-plus"}
      ],
      "pass_env": ["DEEPSEEK_API_KEY", "MOONSHOT_API_KEY", "DASHSCOPE_API_KEY"],
      "permission": {}
    }

- `lead` sits in the planner seat: it may only hand in a list of sub-orders, and any file it writes is left where it is.
- `seats` are the workers. Each gets one OpenCode process per order, started with its own model.
- `pass_env` names the environment variables an agent may see. Everything else in your shell is kept from it.
- `permission` is merged into the OpenCode permission config the agent is started with. A non-interactive
  `opencode run` cannot ask a person, so use `allow` or `deny`, never `ask`.

## What happens to an order

1. Issuing an order sends one `order.issue` envelope to the lead. Saving or cancelling a future plan writes only to the separate plan file.
2. The lead gets a plain folder holding `_dispatch/ORDER.json` (the order, the rules of its seat, the team, the budget)
   and `_dispatch/LIBRARY.json`, and writes `_dispatch/plan.json`: the sub-orders, who gets each, what each may write
   and read, which waits for which, and optionally library uses and skill proposals.
3. Each worker gets a plain folder of its own with no `.git` in it. Only files inside the order's write scope are
   taken; anything else stays in that folder and is listed on the launch (`launches.jsonl` in the run folder, `GET /team`).
4. The judge named on the order rules on what was handed in. Accepted work is merged in the run's git repository and
   tagged under `accepted/`; refused work goes back with the reason, at most once more.
5. When all sub-orders have passed, the lead's order is judged and closed.

The default judge (`cockpit/judge_floor.py`) holds only a floor: something was handed in where the order said, no file
is empty, and each file reads as what its name claims (text is UTF-8, JSON parses, Python compiles; nothing is run).
**It does not look at whether the work is any good.** To hold more than that, write a file with the same three
functions and pass it with `--judge`.

## Safety boundaries, and their limits

- An agent's environment is rebuilt from a short allow-list plus `pass_env`; auto-update and session sharing are
  switched off; the two OpenCode rules that would ask a person are set to `deny`.
- An agent works in a folder of its own, and what it changes outside that folder inside the run folder is recorded on
  its launch. **This is not a sandbox:** an agent that is allowed to run shell commands can reach whatever your user
  account can. Run it in a container or a spare account if that matters.
- The page never inserts text as HTML, and the backend serves four named files and no other path on disk.

## Tests

    for t in tests/test_*.py; do python3 -B "$t" || break; done
    node --check web/app.js
    python3 tools/make_icons.py --check
    python3 tools/make_oneshot.py --check
    python3 tools/privacy_scan.py

`tests/test_page.py` also runs `tests/page_harness.js` when Node is installed. This uses a DOM stand-in, not a browser
or a rendering engine. `tests/test_calendar.py` and `tests/test_library.py` use temporary runs roots outside the
source repository and open no sockets.

`tools/privacy_scan.py` looks through the tracked files (and, with `--history`, the whole git history) for home-folder paths, the local
account name, addresses, keys, session links, AI attribution lines, text that is not English, and picture metadata, and
exits 1 on any hit. It reads text, not pictures, so it cannot see what a screenshot shows.

## Layout

    cockpit/     dispatcher.py (the ledger and its rules), gitws.py (git layer and sandboxes), live.py and serve.py (the
                 running backend), history.py (read-only run replay), planning.py (human-triggered plans),
                 library.py (shared entries, context and ledger-derived learning records),
                 opencode_agent.py (the one place a process is started), judge_floor.py, schema/
    web/         index.html, app.css, app.js, icons.js (generated)
    icons/       the three SVG sources and their licence
    tests/       unit tests including test_calendar.py, page_harness.js (DOM stand-in), a simulator, and the stand-in executable
    tools/       make_icons.py, make_oneshot.py, privacy_scan.py
